## Design Decision: No Hardcoded Equipment Limits

All thresholds that control equipment are user-configurable via Web UI. The only non-configurable values are physical safety limits (`SAFETY_HUM_CEILING_HARD` at 95%) that act as emergency brakes — they don't control normal operation, they prevent disaster.

---

## Design Decision: Alert Rate Limiting

`network_sendAlert()` enforces `NTFY_MIN_INTERVAL_MS` (60s) globally. Alive pings reuse this function and inherit the rate limiting automatically. Critical alerts and alive pings share the same cooldown — alive pings fire every 30 min so collision is unlikely, but if both queue simultaneously, rate limiting applies.

---

## Design Decision: Dormant-by-Default Features

Two v1.4 features ship dormant to prevent unintended behavior on existing installs:
- **`NTFY_ENABLED`** defaults to `false` — alive pings won't fire until the user sets this to `true` and configures their ntfy.sh endpoint
- **`DEFAULT_HUM_EXHAUST_ON`** defaults to `95.0f` (equal to `SAFETY_HUM_CEILING_HARD`) — the high-humidity exhaust won't trigger independently until the user lowers it via Web UI (recommended: 92%)

---

## Design Decision: Alive Ping Behavior

First alive ping fires 30 minutes after boot (not immediately). The boot sequence already sends a "GrowHub32 Online" alert via `network_sendAlert()`. An immediate second ping would be redundant. The alive ping title includes the device MAC address for multi-device identification. HTTP requests are capped at 2000ms timeout to prevent main loop blocking.

---

## Design Decision: Fridge Alarm Thresholds

Fridge temperature alarms fire at 8.0°C (high) and 0.0°C (low/freeze risk) with 10-minute cooldown between repeat alerts. Temperature alerts are suppressed when the fridge heartbeat is lost — disconnect is handled separately by `network_checkFridgeHeartbeat()`. The `-40.0f` sentinel was removed after Kimi review confirmed it's unreachable (ESP-NOW drops packets below -40°C before they reach `g_systemState.fridgeTemp`).
